WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.009
One man’s neuron is another man’s knowledge.

00:00:03.009 --> 00:00:07.640
That’s the stance of the Allen Institute  for Brain Science

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.840
which released the first open access database

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.840
of live human brain cells,

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.120
including data on the electrical properties

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.120
of nearly 300 cortical neurons

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.900
taken from 36 patients.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.040
Plus 3D reconstructions of 100 of those cells.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:28.720
Working with neurosurgeons,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.949
the Institute acquired healthy cells from the cortex

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:34.340
—the outermost layer of the brain—

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.680
from patients undergoing surgery

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.370
for epilepsy or brain tumors.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.320
Normally considered medical waste,

00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:42.809
these rescued tissues will provide scientists

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:43.980
with a unique resource

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:45.980
for understanding the human brain.

00:00:46.149 --> 00:00:50.180
That’s because many studies currently rely on dead

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.179
--not living-- human tissue

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.840
or on brain cells from animals like mice.

